    <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2
    style="
      font-size: 24px;
      line-height: 1.2;
      font-weight: 500;
      margin-top: 2px !important;
      margin: 0;
    "
  >
    The Khamenei dynasty begins
  </h2>
</mj-text>
    <mj-image
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  target="_blank"
/>
    
    <mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What happened? </strong>Mojtaba Khamenei, the 56-year-old son of Iran’s former supreme leader Ali Khamenei, has been selected as <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c78xxg05w0zo" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">Iran’s new supreme leader</a>. Born on 8 September 1969 in Mashhad, he studied at Tehran’s Alavi religious school before later continuing advanced Shia theological studies in Qom. He was accused by reformists of interfering in the 2005 and 2009 elections, which helped hardliner Mahmoud Ahmadinejad rise to and remain in power.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>Controversy:</strong> Critics argue that his rise to power resembles hereditary succession, something the Islamic Republic was designed to prevent after the Iranian Revolution. Reformists and some clerics have long questioned Mojtaba’s religious credentials, noting he is just a mid-ranking cleric. Critics warn his selection could deepen public discontent and reinforce perceptions that Iran’s leadership is becoming increasingly concentrated within one family.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>On the ground:</strong> Global benchmark oil values have <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/mar/09/iran-war-drives-oil-price-above-100-a-barrel-for-first-time-since-2022?utm_source=chatgpt.com" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">surged past $100/barrel</a> for the first time since 2022 as the war in the Middle East disrupts energy distribution and shipping routes. The sudden spike has rattled monetary markets and raised concerns about inflation, fuel expenses, and potential economic slowdowns. The Iranian regime warned that US-Israeli strikes risk pushing values even higher.</p>
</mj-text>
  </mj-column>
</mj-section>
