          <h2
            style="
              padding-bottom: 8px;
              color: #06266d;
              text-align: left;
              border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d;
              font-size: 26px;
              line-height: 1.2;
              font-weight: 300;
            "
          >
            Analysis
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
          padding="10px 12px"
          font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
          color="#000000"
        >
          <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
  Gulf security test
</h2>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Summary: Missile strike near Riyadh heightens Gulf tensions, testing deterrence and regional alliances.</strong></p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>What happened?</strong> Saudi Arabia <a href="https://moderndiplomacy.eu/2026/03/09/saudi-arabia-draws-a-red-line/" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">intercepted missiles and drones</a> aimed at Prince Sultan Air Base near Riyadh, highlighting the widening regional fallout. The attack underscored rising security risks for Gulf states and signaled that strategic military infrastructure in Saudi Arabia is within the conflict’s operational reach.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Reaction:</strong> Iranian President Masoud Pezeshkian issued a rare public apology to Gulf neighbors. Pezeshkian stated Iran would halt attacks on neighboring countries unless those countries were used as launch points for strikes against Iran, and expressed regret for the incidents affecting Gulf states.  However, the apology has been viewed cautiously because missile and drone attacks continued in parts of the Gulf.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;">Moreover, Prince Khalid bin Salman’s meeting with Pakistan’s Field Marshal Asim Munir highlighted Riyadh’s growing defense partnership with Islamabad. The move signalled that Saudi Arabia is not isolated and is strengthening deterrence while maintaining measured rhetoric to prevent reckless regional escalation.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 10px 0 0 0;"><strong>Big picture: </strong>Gulf states that tried to remain neutral are facing pressure to strengthen defenses and coordinate more closely with partners such as the US and Pakistan. At the same time, rising oil values and disruptions to shipping routes underline the economic stakes for the global energy market. Whether the crisis stabilizes will depend on whether deterrence and diplomacy can prevent further strikes.</p>
        </mj-text>
      </mj-column>
    </mj-section>
