            Foundations
          </h2>
        </mj-text>
        <mj-text
  padding="10px 12px"
  font-family="TNYAdobeCaslonPro, 'Times New Roman', serif;"
  color="#000000"
>
  <h2 style="font-size: 24px; line-height: 1.2; font-weight: 500; margin: 0;">
    Namibia’s Caprivi Strip
  </h2>
</mj-text>
<mj-image
  border="1px solid #00000013"
  border-radius="10px"
  padding="10px 12px"
  width="600px"
  src="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/email/images/REPLACE_ME.jpg"
  alt="REPLACE_ME"
  href="https://www.geopoliticalsummary.com/"
/>

<mj-text padding="10px 12px" font-family="Roboto+Serif" color="#000000">
  <p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>What is it?</strong> Namibia’s Caprivi Strip is a narrow, 450 km (269 miles) extension that sticks out from its main territory. It stretches between Angola, Zambia, Botswana, and Zimbabwe. This area is one of the most unique geopolitical regions in southern Africa. The strip serves as a key transit corridor linking various regional economies.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>History:</strong> The <a href="https://www.siyabona.com/caprivi-strip-namibia-history.html" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">Caprivi Strip</a> was created in 1890 by the Heligoland-Zanzibar Treaty, when Germany negotiated a narrow corridor across southern Africa to gain a gateway to the Zambezi River. The river proved unnavigable, but the border stayed. The area later became a transit route during <a href="https://sahistory.org.za/article/liberation-struggle-south-africa" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">southern Africa’s liberation wars</a> and, after <a href="https://sahistory.org.za/article/namibian-struggle-independence-1966-1990-historical-background" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;">Namibia’s 1990 independence</a>, saw a brief secessionist revolt in 1999 by the Caprivi Liberation Army, which was swiftly crushed.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><strong>On the ground:</strong> The Caprivi, known as the Zambezi Region since 2013, is pivotal for tourism, conservation, and cross-border trade. However, its geography also creates security challenges, encourages smuggling routes, and fuels debates over regional autonomy. This unusual stretch of land remains highly influential in Namibia’s politics and economy.</p>
<p style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5; margin: 0 0 10px 0;"><em>Learn more about the Caprivi Strip </em><a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a2O73E2wz14" target="_blank" style="text-decoration: none; border-bottom: 2px solid #06266d; color: black;"><em>here</em></a><em>. </em></p>
</mj-text>
